# The FreeBSD package manifest, with @@VERSION@@ filled in by
# scripts/build-freebsd.sh. UCL, per pkg-create(8).
#
# WHY A .pkg AND NOT A SHELL SCRIPT, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE LINUX ANSWER
#
# installer/linux/install.sh explains at length why Linux gets a script: "a .deb
# reaches Debian and Ubuntu. The same argument then repeats for rpm, for Arch,
# and for Void." That argument is about FRAGMENTATION, and it does not survive
# the crossing. FreeBSD has exactly one package format, and every derivative a
# musician might be running — GhostBSD, NomadBSD, MidnightBSD's cousins — uses
# the same `pkg`. One artifact reaches all of them, `pkg install ./tangent.pkg`
# fetches the dependencies by itself, and `pkg delete tangent` is a complete
# uninstall the system tracks rather than a script the user has to keep.
#
# The tarball path still exists for a no-root install (see install.sh --home),
# because pkg cannot do one: PKG_DBDIR must be owned by root:wheel and there is
# no override, so an unprivileged user cannot register a package at all.
#
# ABI IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT
#
# pkg derives `abi` and `arch` from the build host and enforces them at install
# time: a FreeBSD:14:amd64 package is refused on a FreeBSD:15 host outright.
# Setting `abi = "*"` would dodge the check and is tempting for a single
# artifact — and it is wrong for a package full of native binaries and shared
# library dependencies, because it converts a clean refusal into a runtime link
# failure in front of the user. One artifact per FreeBSD major version instead.
name = "tangent"
version = "@@VERSION@@"
origin = "audio/tangent"
comment = "MIDI keyboard monitor that names what you play in real time"
maintainer = "ganten1998@gmail.com"
www = "https://ganten.neocities.org/projects"
prefix = "/usr/local"
categories = ["audio"]
licenselogic = "single"
licenses = ["MIT"]

desc = <<EOD
Tangent watches a MIDI keyboard and tells you what you are playing: the chord
by name, on the staff, around the circle of fifths, on a guitar neck and in a
Tonnetz lattice, as you play it. It records audio, MIDI and video takes, hosts
LV2 and VST3 plugins and carries its own FM synthesiser, so it makes a sound
on a machine with nothing else installed.

On FreeBSD it reads MIDI straight from /dev/umidi and plays audio through the
alsa-lib-over-OSS shim, so a keyboard and a pair of speakers work with nothing
configured and no sound server running. JACK is offered in the Audio Setup
panel for anyone who wants it, and is not required for anything.

Free, offline, no telemetry, no account.
EOD

# ── Dependencies ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# EVERY ENTRY HERE WAS MEASURED, NOT GUESSED. `procstat -v` on a running Tangent
# gave the 51 shared objects it actually maps, and `pkg which` named the package
# owning each; this is that list, reduced to the leaves (pkg resolves the rest).
#
# What is deliberately NOT here is as important: the build host is GhostBSD,
# which packages the BASE SYSTEM as GhostBSD-clibs, GhostBSD-runtime,
# GhostBSD-zlib and GhostBSD-libexecinfo. Those own libc.so.7, libexecinfo.so.1
# and friends on this machine and own nothing at all on plain FreeBSD, where the
# same files ship in base. Declaring them would produce a package that installs
# only on GhostBSD — the exact opposite of the point, given the tester may be on
# anything.
#
# alsa-lib is the one that decides whether the app STARTS: libasound.so.2 is the
# only ports library the binary links outright, and a stock FreeBSD has no
# reason to have it. alsa-plugins is what makes it make a SOUND — it provides
# libasound_module_pcm_oss.so, which is the shim that turns the ALSA API into
# FreeBSD's native OSS, and the running process maps it.
#
# The X11 and mesa entries look redundant with any desktop and are not: this has
# to work on a machine where somebody installed Xorg and nothing else.
deps {
  alsa-lib      { origin = "audio/alsa-lib";        version = "1.2.16.1"; }
  alsa-plugins  { origin = "audio/alsa-plugins";    version = "1.2.12"; }
  mesa-dri      { origin = "graphics/mesa-dri";     version = "24.1.7"; }
  libglvnd      { origin = "graphics/libglvnd";     version = "1.7.0"; }
  libX11        { origin = "x11/libX11";            version = "1.8.13"; }
  libXcursor    { origin = "x11/libXcursor";        version = "1.2.3"; }
  libXi         { origin = "x11/libXi";             version = "1.8.2"; }
  libXrandr     { origin = "x11/libXrandr";         version = "1.5.4"; }
  libxkbcommon  { origin = "x11/libxkbcommon";      version = "1.13.1"; }
  # The file picker. rfd has exactly two backends here — xdg-desktop-portal and
  # a zenity subprocess — and with neither, `pick_file` returns None, which is
  # indistinguishable from Cancel, so Save/Load/Export silently do nothing.
  # That bug has already been diagnosed once on Linux (docs/LINUX-4.16-FINDINGS)
  # and zenity is the cheap way not to ship it again.
  zenity        { origin = "x11-toolkits/zenity";   version = "4.0.0"; }
  # "Open containing folder", and the desktop-integration triggers that rebuild
  # the menu and icon caches. Those triggers are system-wide and fire for any
  # package writing to share/applications or share/icons — which is why this
  # package needs no post-install script of its own.
  xdg-utils           { origin = "devel/xdg-utils";               version = "1.2.1"; }
  desktop-file-utils  { origin = "devel/desktop-file-utils";      version = "0.28"; }
  hicolor-icon-theme  { origin = "misc/hicolor-icon-theme";       version = "0.18"; }
  # LV2, which is the plugin format FreeBSD actually has. The ports tree
  # carries no VST3 plugins at all, so on this platform LV2 is not a bonus
  # format — it is the only one a user can install from pkg, and a host that
  # cannot read it reaches nothing. `liblilv-0.so` is linked outright by the
  # binary, so this decides whether the app STARTS, exactly as alsa-lib does.
  # Measured with `ldd` and `pkg which`, like every other entry; lilv's own
  # dependencies (serd, sord, sratom, zix) are left to pkg to resolve, which
  # is the leaves rule this list already follows.
  lilv          { origin = "audio/lilv";            version = "0.28.0"; }
  # Video export and import. The app degrades honestly without it — takes still
  # record audio and MIDI — but a DAW that cannot write a video file when the
  # user asks is a support ticket, and ffmpeg is a first-class port.
  ffmpeg        { origin = "multimedia/ffmpeg";     version = "8.1"; }
}

# ── What the user is told after installing ───────────────────────────────────
#
# A `messages` block and NOT a post-install script, deliberately. Users can
# suppress scripts entirely with `pkg add -I` / `--script-no-exec`, and pkg's
# Lua scripts cannot call os.execute at all — so anything load-bearing in a
# script is advice that may never be read. `messages` is the ports-standard
# mechanism, renders as "Message from tangent-x.y.z" at the end of the install,
# and cannot be switched off.
messages [
  {
    type = "install"
    message = <<EOM
Tangent is installed. Run it from the Multimedia menu, or type: tangent

SOUND, WITH NOTHING TO CONFIGURE
  Audio plays through FreeBSD's own OSS by way of the alsa-lib shim. There is
  no sound server to start. If you hear nothing, check the mixer first:

      mixer vol 80 pcm 90

LATENCY, IF YOU CARE ABOUT IT
  One system setting is worth more than every other tweak combined:

      sysctl hw.usb.uaudio.buffer_ms=1     # and add it to /etc/sysctl.conf

  It is the USB audio driver's buffering delay, 4 by default, range 1 to 8.
  Measured here with jack_iodelay on a USB interface, at 1 it takes the round
  trip from 36.7 ms to 27.6 ms and costs nothing in stability. Re-plug the
  interface afterwards -- it applies when a device attaches. (install.sh does
  this for you; this note is for anyone who installed the .pkg directly.)

  For lower still -- 10.9 ms measured -- there is one command:

      pkg install jackit
      tangent-jack start

  It picks the right device, a period the OSS block size actually allows, and
  realtime priority where the system permits it, then `tangent-jack stop` puts
  it away. Choose "JACK" in the Audio Setup panel once it is running.
  Nothing requires it; it is the low-latency option, not a prerequisite.

PLUGINS
  Tangent hosts LV2 and VST3. On FreeBSD that distinction matters: the ports
  tree has no VST3 plugins, so LV2 is where everything is. Anything you
  install from pkg is found automatically the next time Tangent starts --
  there is nothing to configure and no path to set:

      pkg install dragonfly-reverb calf lsp-plugins x42-plugins

  A plugin with its own window opens it inside Tangent. One without a window
  Tangent can embed is still fully usable: every control it publishes appears
  in the automation lanes by name and in its own units -- "Decay, seconds",
  not "parameter 7". Bays are on every channel.

A MIDI KEYBOARD
  Plug it in and Tangent finds it: MIDI is read straight from /dev/umidi, so
  no server or daemon is involved. The device nodes are world-readable by
  default, so no group membership or devfs rule is needed.

  A MIDI port allows ONE reader at a time. If Tangent says a port is busy,
  something else is holding it.

VIDEO
  Recording video needs a Vulkan driver. Hardware is best; mesa's lavapipe
  works on any GPU. Audio and MIDI takes record without it.
EOM
  }
]
